00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:09.759 align:start position:0%
if you miss the days or games like  Diablo or baldur's gate reign supreme in  the PC world fear not because indie  developer think trunk is bringing us all  back to the 90s with their upcoming  series entitled return to games seven  games are planned for the series each  completely different from the last but  with their 90's pc game inspiration  being the connective tissue that holds  them all together the first game in the  series is called book of demons a  card-based hack and slash through the  depths of hell the game itself is  heavily inspired by old PC dungeon  crawlers but there are enough modern  touches that make it feel refreshing to  play in the genre where almost every  other game is trying to emulate the  success of Diablo it's nice to see a  game that comes along and tries to do  something a little bit different the art  style alone is worth mentioning with its  papercraft theme income seen almost  every part of the game characters items  buildings and even the UI are cleverly  designed in a pop-up book style which  the game dubs the paper verse the  environments have a satisfying depth to  them and the unique presentation of the  game saves it from any immediate  comparisons to Diablo or torchlight  because at the very least nothing looks  quite like it  jumping right into the game you'll  notice that you'll be able to choose the  length of your journey the game's levels  are procedurally generated so if you're  short on time you can finish an area in  about five minutes but if you've got the  whole weekend to play you can expand it  to 10 15 minutes or even longer if you  want but one thing that is immediately  apparent when you start the game  regardless of the length of the quest  you choose is that your character is on  a fixed path you can move throughout the  dungeons on your own but only on the  lane that the game designates for you  this may sound like a terrible decision  as most people play dungeon crawlers for  the adventuring aspect but it allows the  game to get creative with how it  presents enemies to you attacking  monsters only takes a single click of  the mouse but when facing a large number  of them you won't be able to just run in  guns blazing you'll need to be  methodical about who you take out and  win unless you get blindsided by a  couple of fireballs thankfully the game  has a fairly robust card-based skill  system so you always have the right  tools to vanquish your foes and trust me  there's no shortage of enemies in this  game the deeper and deeper you dive into  the dungeons the more numerous and more  dangerous they become and even though  the game eases you into learning each  one other attacks and how to counter  them it's still quite a challenge when  you're dealing with a room full of  monsters all at once and it all  culminates in each levels boss battles  which are really the stars of the show  simple but layered in complexity bosses  will test your abilities to multitask  you'll have to manage numerous enemies  magical spells and various different  attack phases in order to defeat them  it's a satisfying experience from one  end of the dungeon to the other and with  three different classes to try out it'll  surely keep players busy for a while  book of demons is bound to entice  newcomers to the genre as well as long  time action RPG fans who have been  longing for a different kind of dungeon  crawler the classic gameplay combined  with the fresh visual look and the  variable difficulty setting promises to  appeal to both ends of the spectrum the  game will be released on steam's early  access platform starting july 28 and  although developer think trunk does plan  on making six more games in the same  vein as book of demons it's no doubt  that they're off to a great start  do you think book of demons and the  return to game series could be a start  of something big tell me what you think  of the game in the comments below and  consider subscribing to gamespot for  more coverage thanks for watching and  I'll see you all next time
 

